        "On a long flight, the steady roar of the engines can make it hard to sleep or even to think. Many travelers now wear headphones that seem to make this noise disappear at the touch of a button. The technology behind them, often called active noise cancellation*, rests on a surprisingly simple idea: they fight sound with more sound.",
        "To understand how this works, it helps to know that sound travels as a wave. When an engine runs, it pushes the air back and forth very quickly, creating areas where the air is slightly squeezed and areas where it is slightly stretched. These changes in pressure reach our ears, and the brain hears them as sound. The faster the air moves back and forth, the higher the sound we hear.",
        "Noise-canceling headphones contain small microphones that listen to the noise around the wearer. A tiny computer inside then produces a second sound wave that is the exact opposite of the first. Where the noise squeezes the air, the new wave stretches it, and where the noise stretches the air, the new wave squeezes it. When the two waves meet, they cancel each other out, and the wearer hears much less.",
